Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 8.2. Evaluation of Acquisition Proposals. <omitted> (c) The Company Board may change its recommendation in favor of the merger if: (i) an Acquisition Proposal is submitted that the Company Board determines, after consultation with its financial advisor and legal counsel, constitutes a Superior Proposal; (ii) the Company Board, after considering factors other than the financial terms, including strategic fit, potential regulatory hurdles, and long-term impact on employees, determines in good faith that such proposal is in the best interest of the shareholders; (iii) the Company has provided the Parent with at least three Business Days' notice of its intention to accept such Superior Proposal, including details of the non-financial aspects considered; and (iv) the Company has negotiated in good faith with the Parent to amend the current agreement to match the benefits of the Superior Proposal, considering both financial and non-financial terms. (Pages 82-83)\nAnswer:
Choices:

