Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is "financial point of view" the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 8.3. Considerations for Superior Proposal. <omitted> (c) Notwithstanding any provision in this Agreement, during the Pre-Closing Period, the Company Board may decide to pursue a Superior Proposal <omitted> if, and only if, <omitted> (ii) the Company Board determines in good faith, after consultation with its outside legal counsel and a financial advisor of nationally recognized standing, that the failure to pursue such a proposal would be inconsistent with the fiduciary duties of the Company Board under applicable Law, (iii) the Company has provided Parent with written notice of the Company Board's intention to pursue a Superior Proposal not less than four Business Days prior to such action, and (iv) if not related to an Intervening Event as per Section 8.3(e), the decision to pursue the Superior Proposal is connected to an Acquisition Proposal or the Company’s intent to terminate this Agreement to enter into a Specified Agreement, ensuring compliance with clauses (1) through (5) as follows: (1) prior to any action, the Company Board shall have determined that such Acquisition Proposal is a Superior Proposal, (2) the Company shall have disclosed to Parent both orally and in writing the key terms and conditions of such Acquisition Proposal and shared all written communications (and, if oral, a summary of the material terms) relating to it as per Section 8.3(b), (3) the Company shall have engaged in good faith negotiations with Parent, and facilitated its Representatives to do so, to the extent Parent wishes to negotiate, during the four Business Day period outlined in clause (iii) of this Section 8.3(c) regarding any proposed revisions to this Agreement or other proposals by Parent, if any, to ensure the Acquisition Proposal no longer qualifies as a Superior Proposal, (4) after evaluating the outcomes of negotiations with Parent and considering the proposals by Parent, if any, in consultation with outside legal counsel and a financial advisor of nationally recognized reputation, the Company Board concludes in good faith that such Acquisition Proposal remains a Superior Proposal, and failing to pursue it would breach the fiduciary duties of the Company Board under applicable Law, and (5) if the Company intends to terminate this Agreement to enter into a Specified Agreement, it must comply with Section 10.1(d)(i). For clarity, this Section 8.3(c) also applies to any amendment to the financial terms or any other material amendment to any Acquisition Proposal (except that any reference to four Business Days shall instead be two Business Days) or any successive Acquisition Proposals. (Pages 82-83)\nAnswer:
